<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yalbugha al-Umari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1366</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Yalbugha al-Khassaki, Sayf ad-Din Yalbugha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yalbugha al-Umari was a senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> emir of the Bahri period who became the strongman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the 1360s. Bought as a mamluk by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> an-Nasir Hasan, he rose to the highest rank of emir of one hundred, and in 1361 he defeated his master, who was never heard from again; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> says Yalbugha had him tortured and killed. As commander in chief under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-yahya-hamid-al-din">al-Mansur Muhammad</a> and al-Ashraf Sha'ban he built a household of some 3,000 mamluks, among them the future sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barquq">Barquq</a>, rebuilt the navy after the Cypriot attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 1365 and patronised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a>. His harsh discipline turned his own mamluks against him, and they killed him in December 1366.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yalbugha's names record his career. The historian Jo van Steenbergen suggests that al-Umari shows he was bought from the Cairene slave trader Umar ibn Musafir, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> an-Nasir Hasan acquired him in 1350; al-Khassaki marks him as a member of the sultan's inner circle, the khassakiyya. After Hasan regained the throne in 1354, Yalbugha returned to his service and, after the murder of the emir Shaykhu in November 1357, was made emir of forty.</p><p>When the sultan arrested the other strongman, Sirghitmish, in August 1358, Yalbugha was promoted to emir of one hundred and made amir majlis, overseeing the sultan's governmental meetings, and he was given Sirghitmish's fortified residence on a hillside overlooking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Hasan's fall Yalbugha and the senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> chose al-Mansur Muhammad, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> an-Nasir Muhammad, and Yalbugha became atabeg al-asakir, sharing power with Emir Taybugha al-Tawil. In the summer of 1361 he marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with the sultan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mu'tadid I against the rebel governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, Baydamur al-Khwarizmi, whose partisans defected. He married Hasan's widow Tulubay, probably to join his household to the Qalawunids, and in 1363 helped replace al-Mansur Muhammad with al-Ashraf Sha'ban. In February 1366 his faction defeated Taybugha's outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><p>After Peter I of Lusignan, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in October 1365, Yalbugha punished the Christian inhabitants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, confiscating valuables and landed property, including some 12,000 crosses. The hundred warships he had built, each carrying 150 sailors, were shown in a ceremony on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in November 1366, but they were never sent against Cyprus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 8 December 1366 an attempt was made on his life while he was hunting outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. His own mamluks, backed by al-Ashraf Sha'ban, rose against him and fought his loyalists on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> with ships of the new navy. Captured on 14 December, he was beheaded by his mamluk Qaratamur as he mounted a horse brought to him in an apparent ruse, and his dawadar Tashtamur buried him in his mausoleum on Rawda Island. The historian Amalia Levanoni explains the revolt by the mamluks' attachment to the laxness of an-Nasir Muhammad's reign, and his fate deterred later Bahri emirs from such reforms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yalbugha al-Umari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
